.TH VIRTPACKER 1 "June 2026" "virtpacker 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
virtpacker \- export and import libvirt virtual machines as portable bundles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B virtpacker
.I list
.br
.B virtpacker
.I export
.RB [ \-o
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-flatten ]
.RB [ \-\-overwrite ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-space\-check ]
.RI [ NAME ]
.br
.B virtpacker
.I import
.RB [ \-\-images\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-nvram\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-tpm\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-owner
.IR USER[:GROUP] ]
.RB [ \-\-new\-mac ]
.RB [ \-\-machine
.IR MACHINE ]
.RB [ \-\-keep\-machine ]
.RB [ \-\-start ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-space\-check ]
.I BUNDLE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B virtpacker
packs a libvirt domain into a single directory containing the domain XML
with host paths templated out, the disk images, the UEFI nvram variable
store, and the swtpm TPM state. The directory is portable and can be
imported on another host.
.PP
On export, disks are copied and by default flattened into standalone qcow2
images. Copies are sparse-aware, so holes in a sparse image are preserved
rather than expanded to zeroes. The nvram file is carried in the bundle and
the domain is switched
to UEFI firmware autoselection so the destination host supplies its own
firmware build. The swtpm state location is read from the domain XML when
an explicit backend source is present, and otherwise taken from the
UUID-derived default directory.
.PP
On import, files are written into the host's standard libvirt directories,
the templated paths are rewritten to their destinations, swtpm state is
placed where the domain expects it, SELinux labels are restored, and the
domain is defined. A versioned machine type is aliased to its generic form
so the destination host's QEMU can supply a supported version.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List the domains libvirt is aware of, with each domain's state and its
disk usage shown as size on disk (actual allocation, honoring sparseness)
and apparent size.
.TP
.B export
Export one domain, or every domain with \fB\-\-all\fR, into bundle
directories under the output directory.
.TP
.B import
Import a bundle directory on the current host.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS export
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-outdir " " \fIDIR\fR
Directory to write bundles into. Default is the current directory.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Export every defined domain.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-flatten
Copy disks as they are rather than flattening. Refuses if a disk has a
backing chain.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Replace an existing bundle directory.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-space\-check
Skip the preflight that verifies the output filesystem has room for the
estimated disk bytes.
.SS import
.TP
.B \-\-images\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Destination for disk images. Default is /var/lib/libvirt/images.
.TP
.B \-\-nvram\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Destination for the nvram variable store. Default is
/var/lib/libvirt/qemu/nvram.
.TP
.B \-\-tpm\-dir " " \fIDIR\fR
Destination directory for an explicit-source swtpm file. Default is
/var/lib/libvirt/swtpm. TPM state using the UUID-derived layout always goes
to its UUID path.
.TP
.B \-\-owner " " \fIUSER[:GROUP]\fR
Ownership applied to installed files. Auto-detected (libvirt-qemu or qemu)
when not given.
.TP
.B \-\-new\-mac
Strip fixed MAC addresses so libvirt regenerates them, avoiding collisions
on a shared segment.
.TP
.B \-\-machine " " \fIMACHINE\fR
Set the machine type for the imported domain. By default a versioned machine
type (such as pc-q35-10.0) is rewritten to its generic alias (q35 or pc) so
the destination host's QEMU resolves a version it supports.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-machine
Keep the bundle's exact machine type instead of aliasing it.
.TP
.B \-\-start
Start the domain after defining it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-space\-check
Skip the preflight that verifies each destination filesystem has room for
the estimated on-disk bytes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bundle/manifest.json
Inventory of the bundle: name, UUID, secure boot flag, disks, nvram, TPM.
.TP
.I bundle/domain.xml.template
Domain XML with paths replaced by placeholders.
.TP
.I bundle/disks/
Disk images.
.TP
.I bundle/nvram/
UEFI variable store.
.TP
.I bundle/swtpm/
TPM state, as a directory or a single file depending on the backend.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Export one domain:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo virtpacker export winvm -o /tank/bundles
.fi
.RE
.PP
Import it on another host and start it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo virtpacker import /tank/bundles/winvm --start
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
Export of nvram and swtpm state and all import operations require root.
The tool assumes system-mode libvirt (qemu:///system).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR virsh (1),
.BR qemu-img (1),
.BR swtpm (8),
.BR restorecon (8),
.BR semanage (8)
